// What people are saying: a snapshot of Slack threads and Jira tickets that bear on CVR, from the last 48 hours only (Thu Oct 29, about 11 AM ET to Sat Oct 31, about 11 AM ET).
const SLACK = [
  { icon: '🛍️', site: 'RB', title: 'Redbubble raises quantity discounts on sticker bundles and art prints', when: 'Oct 30', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0V4PM9DW', refs: [{ go: 'rb|overall', l: 'Redbubble CVR' }, { go: 'rb|revenue', l: 'Redbubble revenue' }], status: 'Live, measuring', tone: 'good', channel: '#released', fx: 'Partly: bigger discounts are meant to lift bundle orders, but no result is posted yet; expect a small effect on CVR and a larger one on sticker order size.', body: 'Ahead of peak, Redbubble raised its quantity discounts: sticker bundles of 10 or more went from 40% to 50% off, and the art print quantity discount went from 15% to 20% off. It went live Oct 29 at about 10 PM ET. The owners will monitor performance; no result has been posted yet.', tldr: 'Sticker bundles of 10+ moved from 40% to 50% off and art print quantity discounts from 15% to 20%, live since late Oct 29.', pts: ['Sticker bundles (10 or more): 40% to 50% off.', 'Art print quantity discount: 15% to 20% off.', 'Live since about 10 PM ET on Oct 29; monitoring has just started.'], qs: ['Does the deeper discount lift bundle orders enough to cover the margin given up?'], why: 'Read Redbubble revenue and orders from Oct 30 on against the week before, since the discount changes order size as well as CVR.' },
  { icon: '🛒', site: 'TP', title: 'TeePublic sticker cross-sell is live; early read is negative but small', when: 'Oct 30', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0C9X6MHV89', refs: [{ go: 'tp|overall', l: 'TeePublic CVR' }, { go: 'tp|revenue', l: 'TeePublic revenue' }], status: 'Too early', tone: 'warn', channel: '#peak_daily_performance_standup', fx: 'No: with about 1,700 engagements it is too early to call, and it does not move the numbers shown here yet.', body: 'The sticker cross-sell launched on Oct 29. Its first data looks negative, but it only has about 1,700 engagements so far and needs more time before anyone reads it. The style selection pills test also had its order value metric corrected: the earlier figure divided revenue by all exposed visitors rather than by orders, so it was swapped for true order value and the old one archived. That test needs a few more days, and desktop is doing better than mobile, so different experiences may follow.', tldr: 'The sticker cross-sell reads negative on about 1,700 engagements, too few to judge; the style pills test needs a few more days.', pts: ['Sticker cross-sell launched Oct 29; the first data is negative on about 1,700 engagements.', 'Style pills test: order value is now measured as revenue divided by orders, and it needs a few more days.', 'Desktop is outperforming mobile on the style pills test, so separate experiences may follow.'], qs: ['How many engagements are enough to call the sticker cross-sell?'], why: 'Do not read the cross-sell as a loss yet; check the A/B tests page in a few days.' },
  { icon: '🤖', site: 'RB', title: 'One machine in Belgium sent about 163K product clicks to Redbubble in a day', when: 'Oct 30', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C095R5DPN3H/p1793383023930509?thread_ts=1793289980.123849&cid=C095R5DPN3H', refs: [{ go: 'rb|overall', l: 'Redbubble CVR' }, { jira: 'DEAP-6942' }], status: 'Fix drafted', tone: 'warn', channel: '#ask-deap-bot-squad', fx: 'Yes, for Redbubble: it behaved like a browser, so Cloudflare scored it as human and it counted in product clicks and pageviews with no add to carts or orders, which pulls CVR down on Oct 27 to 29.', body: 'The Bot Squad traced the "Belgian bot traffic" on Redbubble product pages to a single machine: one Orange Belgium address running Chrome with one fixed browser signature. On Oct 28 it made 99.6% of all Belgian product clicks (about 163K vs about 700 on a normal day) with zero add to carts and zero orders. At the edge it sent about 1.07M requests from Oct 27 to 29, mostly to the search API, and it stopped around 3 PM ET Oct 29. Nothing blocked it because it looked like a real browser and the search API has no rate limit. A rate limit for that endpoint is drafted, starting in log-only mode, and the team wants TeePublic to get the same bot-identifying check Redbubble already has before peak.', tldr: 'A single Belgian machine made about 163K Redbubble product clicks on Oct 28 and no purchases; a rate limit is drafted.', pts: ['One address and one browser signature made 99.6% of Belgian product clicks on Oct 28.', 'About 1.07M edge requests Oct 27 to 29; it stopped around 3 PM ET Oct 29.', 'A log-only rate limit on the search API is drafted (DEAP-6942); TeePublic parity check before peak.'], qs: ['Should Redbubble CVR on Oct 27 to 29 be read with this traffic set aside?'], why: 'A single scraper can move a whole day of Redbubble CVR; expect similar days to read low until the rate limit is enforced.' },
  { icon: '⚾', site: 'TP', title: 'Brewers World Series win lifts TeePublic, softness expected as it cools', when: 'Oct 30', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0C9X6MHV89', refs: [{ go: 'tp|revenue', l: 'TeePublic revenue' }, { go: 'tp|overall', l: 'TeePublic CVR' }], status: 'Cooling', tone: 'good', channel: '#peak_daily_performance_standup', fx: 'Yes: Brewers designs were about 9% of TeePublic revenue on the day they won and about 21% the next day, which lifts orders and revenue; a pull-back is expected Oct 30 and 31.', body: 'Marketing saw a late-night surge in shopping spend on Oct 28 that tracked the Brewers World Series win, and TeePublic revenue grew on Oct 29 because of it. Paid spend got a bump to follow the demand ($5K in one hour) with revenue following the spend. The expectation now is some softness today and tomorrow as Brewers content falls back. For comparison, last year\'s World Series win added only about 3% of revenue the following day, so this is not a trend to count on for next year.', tldr: 'Brewers designs were about 9% of TeePublic revenue on win day and 21% the next, and are now falling back.', pts: ['Brewers content: about 9% of revenue on the day they won, about 21% the next day.', 'Paid spend was raised for an hour to follow the demand; revenue followed.', 'Expect some softness Oct 30 and 31 as it fades.'], qs: ['How much of the Oct 29 CVR lift is Brewers designs vs the rest of the site?'], why: 'Read Oct 29 to 31 TeePublic numbers against non-event days; the Brewers spike inflates the high and then the comparison.' },
  { icon: '🔐', site: 'TP', title: 'TeePublic adds Sign up with Google', when: 'Oct 29', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0V4PM9DW', refs: [{ go: 'tp|overall', l: 'TeePublic CVR' }, { jira: 'DEAP-4840' }], status: 'Live', tone: 'good', channel: '#released', fx: 'Indirect: it is meant to lift account sign-ups, and signed-in shoppers convert better, so any effect would show over weeks, not days.', body: 'Around 1 PM ET on Oct 29, visitors on the default account sign-up flow could sign up with a Google account instead of an email and password. It is not yet offered from Favoriting or Designer Signup. Email sign-up needs a complex password and a confirmation email; Google sign-up removes both. Analytics now records how people sign up (a new sign_up_method property: standard or google). The bet is more signed-in shoppers, who are more likely to buy.', tldr: 'TeePublic shoppers can now sign up with Google from the default sign-up flow, live since about 1 PM ET Oct 29.', pts: ['Live on the default sign-up flow only, not Favoriting or Designer Signup yet.', 'Removes the password rules and the confirmation email step.', 'Sign-ups by method can now be tracked in Amplitude.'], qs: ['Does it show up in sign-up rate within the first week?'], why: 'More signed-in shoppers is a slow CVR lever; check sign-ups by method before reading any CVR change.' },
  { icon: '🏷️', site: 'Both', title: 'November promotions set: TeePublic from Nov 1, Redbubble from Nov 2', when: 'Oct 29', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/CBHK9E864', refs: [{ go: 'tp|overall', l: 'TeePublic CVR' }, { go: 'rb|overall', l: 'Redbubble CVR' }], status: 'Scheduled', tone: 'good', channel: '#promotions', fx: 'Yes: CVR comparisons need matching sale days. TeePublic is off sale today and tomorrow, so Oct 30 and 31 should be compared with non-sale days.', body: 'TeePublic runs a Pre-Cyber stretch from Nov 1 to 23 with a free-shipping coupon on orders of $80 and up and ten sale bursts, each with $16 tees (Nov 1 to 3: up to 40% with $8 off Crewnecks), then November Cyber Nov 24 to 30 with up to 40% and $15 tees. Redbubble runs Pre-Cyber Nov 2 to 21 (up to 50% off) and Cyber Nov 22 to Dec 1 (up to 60% off, with flash deals). On TeePublic the 35% offer moves from Zip Hoodies to Trucker Hats, and sticker and hoodie sale prices were adjusted on Oct 29.', tldr: 'TeePublic Pre-Cyber starts Nov 1 and Redbubble Pre-Cyber Nov 2; Cyber follows on Nov 22 (RB) and Nov 24 (TP).', pts: ['TeePublic: Nov 1 to 23 Pre-Cyber (10 bursts, $16 tees), Nov 24 to 30 Cyber ($15 tees).', 'Redbubble: Nov 2 to 21 up to 50%, Nov 22 to Dec 1 up to 60%.', 'TeePublic sticker and hoodie sale prices were adjusted Oct 29.'], qs: ['How does a $16 tee price in Pre-Cyber compare with this year\'s earlier sales?'], why: 'Sets which days count as comparable; the next sale day starts Sunday on TeePublic.' },
  { icon: '🚨', site: 'RB', title: 'Redbubble data jobs still failing more often than usual', when: 'Oct 29', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0CDJ5GMQN4/p1793252985377989', refs: [{ go: 'rb|overall', l: 'Redbubble CVR' }, { url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0CDJ5GMQN4', l: 'Incident channel' }], status: 'Stabilizing', tone: 'warn', channel: '#inc-1793144113', fx: 'Partly: a failed overnight build can delay a Redbubble day or a shopping feed update; if a recent Redbubble day looks low or missing on this page, check here first.', body: 'The system that runs Redbubble\'s scheduled data jobs is still unstable. Data Engineering measured the impact: task failures are up sharply, but most jobs retry and finish. The one that matters most, the daily warehouse build, failed 4 times in the last week against once the week before. On Oct 29 a fix to a test failure went out and both the daily and the lighter intraday builds finished cleanly. The vendor support case was passed to a specialist team, with an update expected Monday.', tldr: 'Redbubble\'s daily warehouse build failed 4 times in a week vs once before; both builds ran clean on Oct 29, vendor update due Monday.', pts: ['Most data jobs fail more often but retry and finish.', 'Both the daily and intraday warehouse builds completed on Oct 29 after a fix.', 'The vendor case moved to a specialist team; update expected Monday.'], qs: ['When does feed generation finish moving to the new environment?'], why: 'Feeds and reporting depend on these jobs through peak; watch for late Redbubble days and stale feed prices.' },
  { icon: '🕷️', site: 'Both', title: 'Traffic dip looks like a bot-score artifact, not search engines being blocked', when: 'Oct 29', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C095R5DPN3H/p1793196949355329', refs: [{ go: 'tp|channel/Organic Search', l: 'TeePublic Organic Search' }, { go: 'rb|channel/Organic', l: 'Redbubble Organic' }, { go: 'tp|sessions', l: 'TeePublic sessions' }], status: 'Mostly ruled out', tone: 'good', channel: '#ask-deap-bot-squad', fx: 'Reading it correctly matters: crawlers are not shoppers, so they are not in the sessions or CVR on this page. The sitewide "drop" appears only when a very strict bot score (above 90) is used.', body: 'The worry was that Cloudflare\'s new AI crawler controls (Sept 15) were also blocking Google and Bing, which would show up as an Organic Search drop. The follow-up found that the apparent traffic decline mostly disappears when the standard bot score (above 30) is used instead of the stricter cut-offs, so it was a measurement effect. Google and ChatGPT crawlers are steady on both sites; only Claude\'s crawler is blocked, on both sites, a tiny share of crawling. Cloudflare treats Google and Bing as verified bots, so they are not scored as bots. A question is open on whether a bot score above 90 is too aggressive for filtering.', tldr: 'The sitewide traffic drop mostly vanishes with the standard bot score; Google and Bing crawlers are not being blocked.', pts: ['With a standard bot score (above 30) the apparent drop mostly goes away.', 'Google and ChatGPT crawlers are steady; only Claude\'s crawler is blocked.', 'Whether above 90 is too strict a filter is still being discussed.'], qs: ['Which bot-score cut-off should the dashboards use?'], why: 'Keeps a bot-filter choice from being read as an organic search problem; watch Organic Search sessions to be sure.' }
];

